cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you cite a decision, a convention or a working agreement from this record, please cite it as below."
title: "manuals: the decision record and working agreements for V-Sekai"
abstract: >-
  The documentation and decision record behind V-Sekai, a FOSS social
  VR/VRSNS/metaverse stack built on Godot Engine. Published as a Quarto site at
  https://v-sekai.github.io/manuals.

  Holds the Architecture Decision Records in decisions/, dated rather than
  numbered, with superseded entries kept in _archive/decisions/ rather than
  deleted -- a reader who knows which roads turned out to be dead ends is
  better off than one who only knows the current answer. Holds the changelog,
  the reference bibliography, and the working agreements every project in the
  V-Sekai world workspace is written against (CLAUDE.md), which reach the
  workspace root as symlinks declared by the goal manifest.
license: MIT
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
  - name: "V-Sekai Contributors"
    website: "https://github.com/V-Sekai/manuals/blob/main/authors.md"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-28"
repository-code: "https://github.com/V-Sekai/manuals"
url: "https://v-sekai.github.io/manuals"
keywords:
  - social-vr
  - metaverse
  - godot-engine
  - architecture-decision-records
  - working-agreements
  - avatars
  - vrm
references:
  - type: software
    title: "Godot Engine"
    abstract: >-
      The engine everything here is written against. V-Sekai maintains a fork
      at V-Sekai/godot, placed on 3-interactor by the goal manifest, and the
      world build at V-Sekai/world-godot beside it.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/godotengine/godot"
    license: MIT
  - type: software
    title: "VRM: a 3D avatar file format for VR"
    abstract: >-
      The avatar format V-Sekai imports and exports, via V-Sekai/godot-vrm for
      Godot and UniVRM upstream for Unity. Cited as the reason the deployment
      rule in CLAUDE.md is written the way it is: an avatar has to open in a
      client that does not run our code.
    url: "https://vrm.dev/en/"
  - type: software
    title: "git-repo"
    abstract: >-
      The tool that assembles the workspace. V-Sekai/vsk-world-manifest is a
      repo manifest rather than a monorepo checkout: the repositories stay
      independent and separately versioned, and the manifest is the one place
      that says which are in play, on which side, and at which revision.
    repository-code: "https://gerrit.googlesource.com/git-repo"
    license: Apache-2.0
  - type: generic
    title: "Awesome V-Sekai"
    abstract: >-
      The curated index of V-Sekai projects, tools and resources the goal
      manifest was reconciled against. Where the two disagree the manifest is
      authoritative for placement, because placement is what a live manifest
      says.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/V-Sekai/awesome-v-sekai"
